# Transit Calendar — Project Rules

## What this project is

A web-based personal development tool that translates astrological transit data into psychologically grounded, actionable calendar entries. Astrology is the invisible engine. Psychology is the user-facing interface. The calendar is the delivery mechanism.

Three-screen MVP: birth data questionnaire → chart summary → transit calendar ICS download.

Full product spec: `docs/spec.md`. Read the relevant section when starting a new phase. Where the spec and the Decisions section below disagree, the Decisions section wins.

## Decisions (resolved — do not reopen without asking)

- **Framework:** Next.js App Router, TypeScript strict, `src/` directory, npm.
- **Styling:** Tailwind CSS only (v4, tokens defined with `@theme` in `src/app/globals.css`). No CSS modules.
- **Ephemeris:** `astronomy-engine` (MIT, pure JS, runs on Vercel serverless). Do NOT install `swisseph` or `sweph` (native addons, AGPL).
  - All calls to `astronomy-engine` go through one adapter in `src/lib/astro/ephemeris.ts` exposing an `EphemerisProvider` interface, so the backend can be swapped for Swiss Ephemeris later without touching other code.
  - Positions are tropical, geocentric ecliptic longitude of date.
  - Ascendant and MC are computed in our own code from local sidereal time, latitude, and obliquity.
  - House system: Whole Sign. Each house is one full sign, starting from the Ascendant's sign. The unknown-birth-time solar chart uses the same logic with the Sun's sign as house 1.
  - Verify against astro.com or Astro-Seek (both run Swiss Ephemeris) for at least three known birth charts; planets should match within ~0.1°.
- **Place of birth (geocoding):** Open-Meteo Geocoding API (`https://geocoding-api.open-meteo.com/v1/search`), no API key. Use its `latitude`, `longitude` and `timezone` (IANA) fields. Call it through a route handler (`src/app/api/places/route.ts`) so the provider can be swapped later; debounce autocomplete input on the client. Note: the free tier is for non-commercial use; revisit before any paid launch.
- **Timezones:** `luxon`. Convert birth local time to UTC with `DateTime.fromObject({...}, { zone: <IANA zone from the geocoder> }).toUTC()`, never the server's or browser's zone. Handle DST edge cases explicitly:
  - Nonexistent local time (spring-forward gap): detect by round-tripping back to local time and comparing; shift forward and keep a flag.
  - Ambiguous local time (autumn fall-back): use the earlier occurrence and keep a flag.
  - Unit-test both cases plus a historical date (pre-1980) in a zone whose rules have changed.
- **AI:** `@anthropic-ai/sdk`, server-side only. Model name comes from `process.env.ANTHROPIC_MODEL` (default `claude-sonnet-5-5`), never hardcoded. Validate the model's JSON output with `zod` before use.
- **Calendar output:** ICS file (RFC 5545), hand-written in `src/lib/calendar/`, no library. All-day events (`DTSTART;VALUE=DATE`), CRLF line endings, lines folded at 75 octets, commas/semicolons/backslashes/newlines escaped, a stable `UID` per event. Served with `Content-Type: text/calendar; charset=utf-8` and a `.ics` filename. Must import cleanly into Google Calendar and Apple Calendar.
- **Email capture:** Buttondown API, called from a route handler (`src/app/api/subscribe/route.ts`) with `BUTTONDOWN_API_KEY` from env. Show a short consent line next to the field (EU/GDPR), written in brand voice. Do not store emails anywhere else.
- **Fonts (proposed — pending design system pass):** `Cormorant Garamond` for display only (headlines, questionnaire prompts; never below ~20px), `Newsreader` for body, loaded via `next/font/google`.
- **Palette (proposed — pending design system pass):** paper `#F5F0E8`, surface `#EDE6DA`, ink `#2B2622`, muted ink `#6B625A`, hairline `#D8CFC2`, accent (terracotta) `#A0644A`. Use only these tokens; never hardcode hex values in components, so the palette can be replaced in one place.

## Brand voice (applies to all UI copy, microcopy, and loading states)

Honest, warm, slightly confrontational. Like a therapist who skips the pleasantries. Assumes intelligence. Names the uncomfortable thing directly.

- Never use astrological jargon in any user-facing text
- Never use cosmic/mystical language ("the universe is telling you")
- Never use passive hedging ("you may feel"). Use direct statements ("you will feel")
- Never use generic positivity ("trust the process")
- Write in second person
- Astrology terms are allowed only in code, metadata, and internal comments

## Visual design system

- Serif typography — literary, not techy
- Japanese-inspired minimalism: generous whitespace, warm paper tones, restraint
- Palette: off-white and warm neutrals with a single muted accent (tokens above)
- Reference: Kinfolk magazine meets a personal journal
- Mobile-first responsive
- The chart wheel SVG is clean and minimal, not the cluttered default astrology chart look

## Code style and patterns

- API routes (route handlers) handle all server-side logic: chart calculation, AI calls, geocoding, email capture
- Keep components small and single-purpose
- Astrology calculation logic lives only in `src/lib/astro/`
- AI/prompt logic lives only in `src/lib/ai/`
- ICS logic lives only in `src/lib/calendar/`
- Type all chart data, transit objects, and API responses explicitly in `src/types/`
- No `any` types
- Never expose secrets (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, `BUTTONDOWN_API_KEY`) to the client; no `NEXT_PUBLIC_` prefix on secrets

## File structure

```
src/
  app/              # pages and API route handlers
    api/places/     # geocoding proxy (Open-Meteo)
    api/subscribe/  # email capture (Buttondown)
  components/
    ui/             # generic UI (buttons, inputs, transitions)
    chart/          # wheel, summary
    questionnaire/  # form steps
  lib/
    astro/          # ephemeris adapter, chart calculation, timezone conversion, transit engine
    ai/             # system prompt, Claude call, response parsing
    calendar/       # ICS generation
  types/            # shared TypeScript types
docs/
  spec.md           # product spec (reference, not always loaded)
```

## Key architectural decisions

- The AI call is a SINGLE request returning both the chart personality summary AND all calendar entries as structured JSON
- Chart data is a JSON object passed through the flow: form → calculation → AI → display + ICS
- Unknown birth time defaults to a solar chart (sun on ascendant at noon) — handle gracefully in UI, skip house-based detail
- Transit filtering: outer planets (Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune, Pluto) to personal points (Sun, Moon, Mercury, Venus, Mars, Ascendant) only. Major aspects only (conjunction, opposition, square)
- With unknown birth time, exclude the Ascendant from transit targets (it sits on the Sun, so its transits would duplicate the Sun's)
- Email capture is a simple field before download — no account creation

## What NOT to build

Do not add or suggest any of these — they are out of MVP scope:
- User accounts or authentication
- Database or persistence beyond the session
- Payment or premium tiers
- Interactive chart explorer
- Google Calendar API integration (ICS download only)
- Content automation or social posting
- Mobile app
- Dating/synastry features

## Error handling

- AI API failures: retry once, then show a graceful fallback message
- Geocoder failures or no results: let the user retry or rephrase; never block on it silently
- Loading states during AI generation feel intentional ("Reading your chart..."), not broken
- Cache AI responses briefly server-side so a page refresh doesn't re-trigger the API call

## When in doubt

- Simpler is better — this ships in 2–3 weeks
- If it's not in the MVP column of the spec, it doesn't get built
- The questionnaire feels calm and paced, not like a data entry form
- Every piece of user-facing text sounds like the brand voice
- Ask before adding any dependency not listed above
